arXiv:2606.13878v1 [cs.RO] 11 Jun 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration
for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">MoniJesu James
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Marcelino Julio Fernando
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Miguel Altamirano Cabrera
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dzmitry Tsetserukou
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">End-to-end navigation policies trained on large simulation corpora degrade
sharply when transferred to scenes, target categories, or goal modalities
outside their training distribution. The leading zero-shot alternatives
inherit a different but equally constraining ceiling: modular pipelines
such as Modular GOAT are bottlenecked by the recall of a closed-set
object detector, while recent 3D snapshot–memory systems
(e.g. 3D-Mem) accumulate dense, view-dependent representations that are
heavy to maintain and difficult to share across embodiments. We present
<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AnyGoal</span>, a fully decentralized, training-free multi-robot
architecture that places a Vision–Language Model (VLM) at the cognitive
core of frontier-based exploration and coordinates the swarm through a
shared 2D <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gaussian Bayesian Value Map</em> (BVM). The BVM maintains a
per-pixel <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> posterior over goal relevance, updated by
precision-weighted fusion of VLM scores through a depth-cone mask, and
is <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">never reset between subtasks</em>, yielding lifelong evidence
accumulation. Frontiers are ranked by a convex blend of a VLM-as-judge
softmax and a Bayesian Upper-Confidence-Bound (UCB) term computed
directly on the BVM. A sequential greedy allocator with a
spatial-separation penalty and commitment hysteresis distributes
frontiers across agents without any centralized controller. On the full
<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <em id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">val_unseen</em> split (360 episodes,
2,669 subtasks), our dual-agent system achieves <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">52.4 %</span>
Subtask SR at <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12.7 %</span> SPL, state-of-the-art under the
strict physical regime (discrete <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25\,\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25\,\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> steps, no
teleportation, <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>42</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> HFOV) and a <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+27.5 percentage-point</span>
improvement over the published Modular GOAT baseline (24.9 %). The
single-agent AnyGoal configuration alone achieves
<span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">41.9 %</span> Subtask SR, demonstrating that the gains arise from
the underlying decision architecture rather than from raw agent count.
A controlled four-way perception ablation shows that
<em id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-recall open-vocabulary detectors shift the dominant failure
mode of zero-shot navigation away from exploration and onto goal
verification at the stopping decision</em>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Multi-Modal Navigation, Vision-Language Models, Multi-Agent
Coordination, Bayesian Mapping, Zero-Shot Embodied AI</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Embodied agents that operate in real homes will be asked, in a single
deployment, to find an object by name, by photograph, and by free-form
natural language description, all while persisting useful memory across
tasks. <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> crystallized this
requirement as <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-modal lifelong navigation</em>: an agent receives a
stream of <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> heterogeneous subtasks per episode in photorealistic
HM3D scenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, with no scene or category
supervision at training time. Two regimes have emerged on this benchmark.
End-to-end policies, most prominently SenseAct-NN Skill
Chain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and the concurrent
AstraNav-Memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, attain strong path efficiency
on familiar distributions, but their reliance on learned spatial priors
makes them brittle under category, modality, and embodiment shift. Modular zero-shot
pipelines such as Modular GOAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> side-step training by
composing off-the-shelf detection, mapping, and planning stacks, but inherit
the <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closed-vocabulary</em> ceiling of detectors such as
DETIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>; on <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">val_unseen</em> the
strongest Modular GOAT variant attains <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.9\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.9</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.9\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> Subtask SR and
<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.2\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.2\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> SPL. A more recent line of work, exemplified by
3D-Mem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, replaces the implicit detector with a
view-dependent 3D snapshot database queried by a VLM. This restores
open-vocabulary recall at the cost of substantial memory and compute
overhead, and remains structurally single-agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that neither extreme is necessary. The <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spatial</em> reasoning
that modular systems delegate to fragile detectors and the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic</em>
reasoning that 3D systems delegate to per-snapshot VLM calls can be unified
inside a single lightweight 2D structure, a <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gaussian Bayesian Value
Map</em>, if the VLM is treated as a noisy oracle whose answers are fused into
a persistent spatial posterior. Once frontiers carry calibrated
<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> belief, they admit a principled blend of
<em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exploitation</em> (VLM relevance) and <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exploration</em> (UCB on the BVM),
and they become a natural medium for decentralized multi-agent allocation:
each agent reads the same map but acts greedily under a separation penalty
that no centralized scheduler has to enforce.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AnyGoal</span>, a fully decentralized, training-free
multi-robot architecture for multi-modal lifelong navigation, and validates
it through the most comprehensive perception ablation reported on
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">val_unseen</em> to date. Concretely:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.4em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Gaussian Bayesian Value Map for lifelong relevance.</span>
We replace the Beta-Bernoulli and binary occupancy heuristics common in
zero-shot navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> with a
precision-weighted Gaussian update over per-pixel <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
goal-relevance belief, fused through a depth-cone observation mask. The map
is never reset between subtasks, supporting cross-subtask evidence
carry-over and natural hallucination decay.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convex-blended VLM-as-judge with Bayesian UCB.</span>
Candidate frontiers are rendered as A/B/C/D markers on the egocentric view;
the VLM returns a softmax. We blend the softmax with a UCB term on the
BVM via a convex coefficient (<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\!=\!0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\!=\!0.50</annotation></semantics></math>), gated by an explicit
“is-worth” Yes/No prompt (<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\!0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\!0.50</annotation></semantics></math>). This recovers the ergodic
exploration guarantees of UCB while letting the VLM dominate when its
preference is sharp.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decentralized swarm coordination without explicit messaging.</span>
Frontier allocation is a sequential greedy bid over the shared BVM with a
spatial-separation penalty
(<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d\!&lt;\!100\,\mathrm{px}\!\Rightarrow\!\times\!0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>px</mi><mpadded style="width:0.830em;" width="0.830em"><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!&lt;\!100\,\mathrm{px}\!\Rightarrow\!\times\!0.6</annotation></semantics></math>) and a
<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\!\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\!\times</annotation></semantics></math> commitment hysteresis. No centralized controller and no
explicit inter-agent message channel are required beyond the shared map.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Headline results on <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span></span>.
On the full <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">val_unseen</em> split (360 episodes, 2,669 subtasks),
AnyGoal with <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> ground agents achieves <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">52.42 %</span> subtask SR
and <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12.66 %</span> SPL, exceeding Modular GOAT by <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+27.5 pp</span>
and the strongest zero-shot single-agent system TANGO by <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+20.3 pp</span>
on subtask SR. Our <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!1</annotation></semantics></math> baseline already attains <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">42.0 %</span>
subtask SR, demonstrating that the architectural prior, not raw agent
count, is doing most of the work.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A scientific characterization of the perception bottleneck.</span>
A four-way ablation across SAM3, SAM3+DINOv2, GroundedSAM, and
YOLO-World+MobileSAM, with detector quality varied and every other
component held fixed, shows that the dominant failure mode of zero-shot
navigation <em id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shifts</em> as recall increases: high-recall SAM3 fails
through premature <span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">stop</span> calls (<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80.2\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80.2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80.2\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> of failures), while
low-recall YOLO-World exhausts its step budget (<math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42.3\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>42.3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42.3\,\%</annotation></semantics></math>). This
reframes the open problem from “find better detectors” to “verify the
goal at the stopping decision”.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper develops these claims.
Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> positions AnyGoal against the closed-set
modular line, the 3D snapshot-memory line, and centralized multi-agent
reinforcement learning; Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> details the BVM, the
convex-blended UCB score, and the decentralized allocator;
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental Setup ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the evaluation protocol;
Sections <a href="#S5" title="5 Results and Evaluation ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>–<a href="#S6" title="6 Discussion ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> present the
state-of-the-art result, the multi-agent scaling curve, the four-way
perception ablation, and a discussion that interprets SPL across
simulation regimes; Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Limitations and Future Work ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> states the remaining
limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-modal lifelong navigation and closed-set perception.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> consolidates object-, image-,
and language-goal navigation into a single lifelong protocol on
HM3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Its reference baselines, Modular GOAT
(classical SLAM<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>DETIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>) and SenseAct-NN Skill
Chain, both rely on <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closed-set</em> vocabularies, and the benchmark
attributes their ceiling to detector recall on out-of-distribution
categories. Open-vocabulary backbones
(GroundingDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, YOLO-World <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>) relax this at the
detection level, but systems that bolt them on without a memory of
<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> the goal was last plausible remain susceptible to premature
stops on near-duplicates (§<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Perception bottleneck ablation (the 4-way test) ‣ 5 Results and Evaluation ‣ AnyGoal: Vision-Language Guided Multi-Agent Exploration for Training-Free Lifelong Navigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>). AnyGoal
back-projects every detection through depth so spatially inconsistent VLM
hits are rejected before reaching the planner, and treats the VLM not as
a categorical oracle but as a noisy posterior fused into a calibrated map.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory in embodied AI.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Navigation memory has moved from semantic occupancy
grids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> to 3D snapshot representations.
3D-Mem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> is the strongest recent exponent: a
<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-snapshot</em> 3D-fragment database re-queried by a VLM at every
decision point (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.6\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>49.6</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.6\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> Episode SR, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.8\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.8</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.8\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> SPL). The representation
is single-agent and view-dependent, and the cost of re-rendering snapshots
through a VLM grows linearly in episode length and quadratically with
agent count. The BVM takes the opposite stance, a 2D per-pixel Gaussian posterior
whose storage is constant in episode length and whose merge across
agents is a closed-form precision-weighted sum, placing AnyGoal in the
direct lineage of value-map navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>
but with <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uncertainty</em> as a first-class quantity that drives both
UCB exploration and the rejection of spatially inconsistent detections.
VLFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> is the conceptual ancestor of this line
but reports only on single-modality <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ObjectNav</span> and has no
published <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GOAT-Bench</span> <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">val_unseen</em> number; we therefore
do not include it as a direct baseline and instead position the Gaussian
BVM as its principled multi-modal, uncertainty-aware extension.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zero-shot and multi-agent robotics.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Zero-shot semantic navigation has progressed via VLM-grounded frontier
methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>,
LLM-planned skill chains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, graph
captioning pipelines such as TANGO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and
unified-goal frameworks such as UniGoal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>; trained
counterparts include MTU3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. All are overwhelmingly
single-agent. Multi-agent navigation is dominated by
centralized MARL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> that requires training,
reward shaping, and tight communication assumptions. We instead reduce
coordination to a <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decentralized cost-utility bid</em> over the shared
BVM, in spirit closest to classical decentralized
auctions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> but operating over
an uncertainty-aware value map rather than a flat information-gain field,
with no explicit messaging beyond map synchronization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:72.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-118.3pt,24.9pt) scale(0.593159443868947,0.593159443868947) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="169.16" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 804.8 169.16" width="804.8"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,169.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(61.19,0) translate(0,138.19)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#737373;--ltx-fill-color:#F7F7FF;" fill="#F7F7FF" stroke="#737373" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path d="M 608.54 30.7 L 262.32 30.7 C 260.03 30.7 258.17 28.84 258.17 26.55 L 258.17 -109.99 C 258.17 -112.28 260.03 -114.14 262.32 -114.14 L 608.54 -114.14 C 610.83 -114.14 612.69 -112.28 612.69 -109.99 L 612.69 26.55 C 612.69 28.84 610.83 30.7 608.54 30.7 Z M 258.17 -114.14"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 269.98 -41.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:23.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="330.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:23.91em;">
